WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:00.790
IVAN RAMIREZ: Hey, Fei.

00:00:00.790 --> 00:00:01.195
How's it going?

00:00:01.195 --> 00:00:02.125
FEI XUE: Hi, Ivan.

00:00:02.125 --> 00:00:02.350
IVAN RAMIREZ: Wow.

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:04.224
These micro-kitchens
sure are getting bigger.

00:00:04.224 --> 00:00:05.807
FEI XUE: I know,
so much chocolate.

00:00:05.807 --> 00:00:07.390
IVAN RAMIREZ: Anyway,
machine learning

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:08.711
has been all the craze lately.

00:00:08.711 --> 00:00:11.210
And we're looking for better
and faster ways to leverage it.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.514
But we often run into
operation overhead,

00:00:13.514 --> 00:00:15.430
especially considering
all the time and effort

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:17.290
it takes to develop
a specific model

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:19.130
and having it run
in different places.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:19.713
FEI XUE: Yeah.

00:00:19.713 --> 00:00:21.370
I actually hear
this issue a lot.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.920
A common scenario is
that a data scientist

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.400
would develop his or
her model on a laptop,

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.800
train that model in the cloud,
then serve it in production,

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.080
maybe on prem or in
a different cloud.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.510
In these different environments,
software and hardware

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:37.110
configurations could
totally be different.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.400
That makes things break
easily when moving from one

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.910
environment to the other.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.630
So what if I told you
that there would be a way

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.330
to easily develop, train,
and deploy machine learning

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:49.890
models in a very scalable and
flexible way by leveraging

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:50.562
Kubernetes?

00:00:50.562 --> 00:00:52.270
IVAN RAMIREZ: Wow,
that would be amazing.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.040
Do you think you could show us
all of that in five minutes?

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:54.789
FEI XUE: For sure.

00:00:54.789 --> 00:00:57.530
I'll be using an open source
framework to do just that.

00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:58.780
It is called Kubeflow.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:02.110
It helps you run machine
learning models on Kubernetes.

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.590
Well, I will show you how to
use Kubeflow to first train

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.160
a TensorFlow model to recognize
handwritten numbers then

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.950
to serve that model on
an application server.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.830
On top of that, we will
deploy a web interface

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.710
to interact with the model
over public internet.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.110
So Kubeflow actually supports
distributive training,

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:21.860
as well as training on GPUs.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.860
But to keep things
simple today, I

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.974
will demo CPU-only
training on a single node.

00:01:26.974 --> 00:01:27.765
Let's jump into it.

00:01:27.765 --> 00:01:29.674
IVAN RAMIREZ: Let's do it.

00:01:29.674 --> 00:01:32.014
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.560
FEI XUE: I have already spun
up a Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.440
Kubeflow uses a tool
called ksonnet to help

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.410
manage Kubernetes
development packages.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:47.350
To get started, first I
deploy Kubeflow component

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:49.480
to my Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.540
Some of these components
include a Jupiter hub

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.950
for model development and TF
Job for managing training jobs.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.380
After those are deployed,
I can run Kube Control

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.060
Get All and see a list of
all the resources provisioned

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:08.720
by Kubeflow.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.110
Now that Kubeflow
is ready to go,

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.050
let's start training
in the model.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.720
It's actually really easy.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.560
I have already built a container
with existing Python code

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.930
and pushed it to the
Container Registry.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.910
This Python code
trains the model

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.730
to recognize handwritten
digits then export

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.320
the model to a storage bucket.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.960
Now I will deploy the
training component.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.750
I set the parameters
for the training drop

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.060
to tell it where to find the
container in the registry.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.400
You can now see in
the Workflow page,

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:50.370
I have a container being created
to perform the training job.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:54.830
When it is done, the model
will be exported to a bucket.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:58.100
It is going to take a little
while spinning up the container

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.810
and doing the actual training.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.480
While this is happening,
let's set up a server

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.220
to serve the model.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.970
I'm using a TF Serving
deployment to do it.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.680
I will point the
server to the bucket

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.980
where our model
will be exported to.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.900
Now, in the Workload
page, you can

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:21.010
see that I have a serving
deployment called Minstserv.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:23.290
After that, let's
also deploy a web app

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:26.590
UI to interact with the model.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:29.260
Here I have a simple
Flask app that

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.300
will connect with
the server that we

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:34.060
deployed in the last step.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.270
Again, I have packaged
it into a container

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.590
and pushed it into the registry.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:45.670
I use ksonnet to deploy
it with an external IP.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.160
Back to the Workflow
page, you can see the web

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.320
UI deployment is also running.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.280
Now let's check on the
status of our training job.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:55.390
It is still running.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:57.450
Let's look at the logs.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:01.420
OK, it is almost done,
almost 90% there.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.240
Now we can click on the
Refresh button here.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:06.640
You can see the
training is now over,

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.800
and the model has been
uploaded to the bucket.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.460
With that, we can
play with our model.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.399
Let's go to our
web app component,

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:21.850
find the external IP,
connect to the server.

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:23.020
Here it is.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:28.180
We can then use that server
to test on some random images.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.960
So the first one, we're seeing
a picture of a handwritten zero,

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:36.300
and it's giving the correct
predictions of zero.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:40.150
With that, let's test on
couple of other random images.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:43.090
We can see that it's
giving the correct result.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:47.370
So everything we just saw today,
from training, to surveying,

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:51.930
to web app UI, is all
happening on GKE Kubernetes

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.490
with Kubeflow.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.240
IVAN RAMIREZ: Well,
Fei, that was amazing.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:57.230
FEI XUE: Well, thanks.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:00.860
Well, now you can easily
develop, train, and deploy

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.500
your machine learning models
everywhere on Kubernetes,

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.090
thanks to Kubeflow.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.460
I want to give a big shout
out to Dan [? Senche, ?]

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.840
who developed the demo material.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.219
Please like, comment,
and subscribe down below.

00:05:12.219 --> 00:05:14.510
IVAN RAMIREZ: And if your
company's working on any cool

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:16.551
projects and you'd like
to reach out to the team,

00:05:16.551 --> 00:05:19.010
go ahead and click on the
link down below as well.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

